The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The table it renders under
those words says something else: two causes, one of them marked as having no ticket of its own,
and a footnote counting reports the desk read as a one-day move. Reading every report body in
the export gives three problems, each with its own fix. They are now three tickets.

<Callout kind="warn">
The words on a kb page are a claim. The tables are built from the export at render time. When they
disagree, trust the table, then fix the words.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**Parser reads month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day first, and the shared parser
reads every date month first, so `7 April` goes out as `4 July`. It shows up on every channel and
every tenant. Fix: parse with the tenant's locale, which `causes.ts` already knows how to ask the
platform for.

**The booking-v3 hint tells people to type month first.** Under its date field, booking-v3 prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`. People do what the hint says, and the form reads the field the tenant's way, so the
same swap appears for a different reason. Only the three tenants on booking-v3 see it, and only
through the form. Fixing the parser will not fix this; the hint or the form must change.

**A one-day move.** A handful of reports describe no swap at all: the month is right and the day is
off by one, or two clients in different time zones got different days for the same meeting. That
is a time-zone boundary, not a date format. The desk read these correctly and noted them in
`desk-notes.json`, then filed them under the parser by tenant. The table above counts them the
same way, inside the parser row, and gives them a ticket of their own so they stop waiting for a
fix that cannot touch them.

## Why one ticket held all three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone. The twelve
subject lines in this ticket are shared across all three problems, so subject matching could not
tell them apart. The intake tag could not help either: it is a guess from the reporter's browser
locale, and it disagrees with the tenant directory on a meaningful share of reports, in both
directions. The one place that knew the difference was the report body, which the rule says nobody
has to read.

## Lessons

- A status of `known` describes the words on the page, not the data under them. Read the rendered
  tables before trusting a Callout.
- A footnote with a count in it is a ticket nobody filed yet.
- Merging on subject is fast, and it hides bugs that share a symptom. When a merged ticket passes a
  few hundred reports, sample the bodies before calling it one fix.

